    "text": "**Business & market dynamics:** Norwegian Cruise Line is seeing solid demand-driven top-line expansion, but pricing power remains under pressure as net yield guidance was lowered. European booking headwinds and fuel-cost volatility have recently weighed on margins. The narrative is shifting, however, as a U.S.–Iran preliminary peace agreement is expected to ease energy prices, offering tangible margin relief. Geopolitical risks linger, yet the operating backdrop is becoming more favorable, with falling fuel costs providing a near-term catalyst amid still-solid cruise demand.\n\n**Management strategy and execution:** Management’s credibility has been dented by a cut to full-year earnings guidance, sparking market skepticism about the outlook. In response, the company announced significant SG&A savings to restore margins, with most of the benefit flowing through this year and fully realized in 2027, which leadership describes as a transition year. An activist pact (Elliott) adds external pressure to improve capital allocation. The combination of cost initiatives and activist engagement frames a turnaround effort, though lingering distrust around guidance tempers the narrative.",
